class:: MultiPitch
summary:: Estimate several simultaneous harmonic fundamentals
categories:: UGens>Analysis>Pitch
related:: Guides/MIR2, Classes/Pitch2, Classes/FundamentalCandidates

description::
An independent Klapuri-style iterative estimator based on “Multiple Fundamental Frequency Estimation by Summing Harmonic Amplitudes” (ISMIR 2006, DOI 10.5281/zenodo.1416740). Hann frames are zero-padded to twice their length. Thirty critical-band triangles estimate whitening gains with exponent -0.67. Harmonic weights are (f0 + 52) / (harmonicFrequency + 320); candidates are selected, their Hann main-lobe neighbourhoods are suppressed, and extraction stops when sum(selectedSaliences) / count**0.7 decreases. MIR2 uses a bounded log-frequency grid, at most 80 partials, 0.9 cancellation, and a spectral-flatness gate; this changes the paper's continuous period search and reconstructed partial cancellation. Close notes, octave-related sources, dense chords, percussion and inharmonic sounds remain difficult. The first implementation is experimental and does not identify separate instruments.

Like link::Classes/Pitch::, this family extracts control signals from sound; it exposes explicit evidence or contour information for synthesis control.

classmethods::
method:: kr
argument:: in
Audio-rate mono signal. Use one analyzer per channel; mix deliberately before analysis when appropriate.

argument:: maxPolyphony
Literal capacity from 1 through 6. Unfilled frequencies and strengths are zero.

argument:: minFreq
Initialization-time lower frequency bound in Hz. Time-domain methods need at least two periods in frameSize; SWIPE benefits from eight periods.

argument:: maxFreq
Initialization-time upper frequency bound in Hz, greater than minFreq and below Nyquist.

argument:: frameSize
Literal power of two from 256 through 8192. First result follows one full frame. SWIPE uses centred Hann windows from 256 samples through this maximum, aligned to the centre of the trailing frame.

argument:: hop
Literal frame fraction from 0.0625 through 1. Updates every round(frameSize * hop) samples; this is not seconds.

argument:: method
The available method is \klapuri, a documented iterative adaptation.

argument:: threshold
Stop when the next residual score is below this fraction of the initial score, from 0 through 1; the polyphony objective also stops extraction.

argument:: binsPerOctave
Literal integer from 12 through 96. Spectral candidate frequencies are minFreq * 2 ** (index / binsPerOctave); higher density costs CPU and initialization memory.

returns::
An Array containing frequency Array, relative-strength Array, and detected count. Strength is residual salience relative to the first selected source, not probability.

examples::
MultiPitch implements an iterative spectral subtraction harmonic summation algorithm adapted from Klapuri (ISMIR 2006) to estimate multiple concurrent fundamental frequencies. In this example, it analyzes a polyphonic mixture of walking bass and treble melody: the two detected F0 streams independently synthesize an analog-style resonant bass voice and a sparkling treble lead. An interactive code::MIR2GUI.pianoRoll:: plots the primary tracked melody line.
code::
(
s.waitForBoot({
    var path = MIR2GUI.soundPath("bass-melody-mix.wav");
    ~mir2Buffer = if(path.notNil) { Buffer.read(s, path) } { nil };
    ~mir2Bus = Bus.control(s, 1);
    s.sync;
    ~mir2Example = {
        var source = if(~mir2Buffer.notNil) {
            PlayBuf.ar(1, ~mir2Buffer, BufRateScale.kr(~mir2Buffer), loop: 1)
        } {
            // Synthetic bass + melody fallback
            var bass = SinOsc.ar(Select.kr(LFNoise0.kr(1).range(0, 4), [55, 65.4, 73.4, 82.4]), 0, 0.3);
            var lead = Saw.ar(Select.kr(LFNoise0.kr(3).range(0, 5), [220, 261.63, 293.66, 329.63, 392]), 0.15);
            bass + lead
        };
        // For live input instead: source = SoundIn.ar(0);

        var result = MultiPitch.kr(source, maxPolyphony: 2, minFreq: 55, maxFreq: 1200, frameSize: 2048, hop: 0.25);
        var freqs = result[0];
        var strengths = result[1];

        // Bass voice from lower candidate, melody voice from upper candidate
        var bass = SinOsc.ar(freqs[0].clip(40, 400).lag(0.03), 0, strengths[0].lag(0.04) * 0.2 * (freqs[0] > 40));
        var melody = Saw.ar(freqs[1].clip(150, 2000).lag(0.03)) * strengths[1].lag(0.04) * 0.12 * (freqs[1] > 100);
        var sound = (source * 0.2) + bass + melody;

        if(~mir2Bus.notNil) { Out.kr(~mir2Bus, freqs[0]) };
        sound * Line.kr(0, 1, 0.2) ! 2
    }.play;

    ~mir2GUI = MIR2GUI.pianoRoll("MultiPitch Tracker", ~mir2Bus, 36, 84);
});
)
::
code::
(
~mir2Example.free;
if(~mir2GUI.notNil) { ~mir2GUI.close };
if(~mir2Bus.notNil) { ~mir2Bus.free };
if(~mir2Buffer.notNil) { ~mir2Buffer.free };
)
::

